<think>
The problem asks whether we can remove exactly $k$ characters from a given string $s$ of length $n$ such that the remaining characters can be rearranged to form a palindrome.

First, let's recall the condition for a string to be rearrangeable into a palindrome. A string can be rearranged into a palindrome if and only if at most one character appears an odd number of times in the string. All other characters must appear an even number of times.

We are given the original string $s$ and an integer $k$. We need to remove exactly $k$ characters. Let the remaining string have length $m = n - k$.
Let the counts of each character 'a' through 'z' in the original string be $c_a, c_b, \dots, c_z$.
When we remove characters, we decrease these counts.
